WAN Failover Plugin

Decides when an IPv4 WAN leaves service and when it comes back, moves
connections to the right WAN both ways, and restarts Tailscale only when
the default route actually changed.

- A WAN whose dpinger loss exceeds the gateway's own threshold, or that is
  hard down, is held with force_down while another WAN is usable; once
  held it returns only when a settled window shows no loss at all
- Never both: a hold needs a usable alternative and is released at once
  on positive evidence that the alternative failed
- A reboot releases the holds the plugin took before routing starts
- A new hold kills that WAN's states; when a WAN recovers, flows that
  prefer it move back once their rule and the default route point there
- Ownership is a record of the boot that took each hold, saved with
  force_down in one config write; only a hold its record proves is ever
  released

Plugin Changelog
================

1.4_3

* The source moved to github.com/LegoTypes/plugins; no change to
  the plugin.

1.4_2

* Reinstalling the package after a removal brings its minute job back

1.4_1

* Removing the package also removes its minute job

1.4

* The list of gateways the plugin holds is no longer part of the settings,
  so the settings API cannot change it. A hold is released only when the
  plugin can prove it took it in this boot (or, at boot, the one before);
  any other record, such as a backup's from another boot, is pruned and
  logged, and its gateway stays forced down until a person unticks Force
  down. On upgrade, the holds 1.3_1 took in this boot are carried over,
  as the plugin's own state file proves them; an entry of 1.3_1's list
  it does not prove, such as one a restored backup brings back, is
  dropped and its gateway stays forced down
* A hold's state kill selects the states routed via that WAN's gateway and
  interface, by state id; two gateways sharing an address no longer lose
  each other's connections, and the log reports each kill per gateway and
  interface
* Removing the package releases its holds and re-syncs routing; upgrades
  keep them
* A settings save or a restored configuration that moves force_down under
  the plugin's feet aborts the plugin's write instead of overwriting it;
  the next evaluation plans again
* Runtime, state and trace files are root-only; the state file's shape is
  checked when it is read, and gateway and interface names from the
  configuration are checked where they are used
* Status page text is decoded without an HTML parser, and the status
  notice shows each note on its own line
* The crash-test hook is a root-only flag file, not an environment
  variable

1.3_1

* A young gateway monitor's lost probes are counted as whole probes:
  dpinger prints loss rounded to 0.1 %, so a WAN that had lost exactly
  the high-loss count (12 of 36 reads 33.3 %, 11.988 probes) was judged
  bad only after it lost one more probe or the monitor settled

1.3

* A WAN whose link is down (no carrier or no IPv4 address) has no gateway
  monitor, by design: core stops it with the address. That no longer
  reads as core changing underneath the plugin, which blocked new holds
  and, after 180 s, released every hold; with a flapping WAN released,
  core kept electing it and installed no IPv4 default route at all.
  Such a WAN is held as unavailable, and the hold stays while the link
  is down
* A DHCP WAN loses its gateway address with its link, and its hold ran
  the state kill without an address (pfctl usage error, nothing killed):
  connections stayed pinned to the dead WAN. The kill now uses the last
  gateway address the WAN had, and is skipped and logged when none was
  ever seen

1.2

* Failback delay (default 10 minutes): connections move back to a
  recovered WAN only after it has read clean that long; any loss restarts
  the wait, and a WAN held again never gets them back. Fail back now
  (button or configctl wanfailover failback_now) ends the wait

1.1

* Failback recognises a gateway group rendered as a pool that names one
  gateway several times (its weight); it was taken for a balanced rule,
  so flows preferring that gateway never moved back

1.0

* Initial release
